  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "noteIntro",
      "type": "NoteNode",
      "x": -40,
      "y": -183,
      "init": {
        "label": "A tiny transformer",
        "color": "violet",
        "collapsed": false,
        "width": 820,
        "height": 560,
        "body": "# A tiny transformer\n\nThe forward pass of a language model, the same arithmetic as GPT at toy size: one layer, one attention head, four numbers per word and a vocabulary of five. Read it left to right; each step is a card, and each stage ends in a picture of what it made.\n\n1. **Read the prompt.** Each word of *the cat sat on* is found in the vocabulary and becomes a one-hot row: all zeros but a 1 under its word.\n2. **Embed.** The one-hot rows pick each word's four numbers out of the embedding table, and each position adds its own.\n3. **Attend.** Each word asks with its query and answers with its key. A query times a key, over √4, scores how much one word should look at another; the mask hides the words that come after, and a softmax turns each row into weights that sum to 1. The weights mix the values, and the mix is added back. Here *sat* and *on* put nearly all their attention on *cat*.\n4. **Think.** A two-layer network with a ReLU works on each word by itself, and its answer is added back too.\n5. **Predict.** The unembedding scores every word of the vocabulary, and a softmax makes the scores probabilities. Each position predicts the word after it: *the* expects *cat* or *mat*, *cat* expects *sat*, *sat* expects *on*, and *on* expects *the*.\n\nThe weights were set by hand to tell that story; a real model learns billions of them from text. Two things to try: shorten the prompt to *the cat sat* and watch *on* become the next word, or set the 3 in W_K's second row to 0 and watch the attention on *cat* spread out.",
        "fieldTypes": {}
      }
    },
